  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Study on Potential of Circular Proteins for Animal Feed in Tanzania</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The limited availability and high costs of raw materials severely constrain the production of quality feeds in Tanzania and East Africa. Feed manufacturers often use poor-quality raw materials, resulting in lower-quality feeds, causing suboptimal animal performance and leading to mistrust.Sourcing alternative protein ingredients can potentially solve this issue. Alternative protein ingredients are easily accessible, like waste streams and by-products, but specific knowledge on the potential and the application of these ingredients is lacking.This study aims to lower costs and improve the quality and circularity of animal feeds produced in Tanzania by providing insights into the nutritional, technical and commercial potential of alternative protein ingredients for animal feed in Tanzania. Using protein alternatives, including waste streams, will enable the feed sector to become fully circular.The insights and recommendations from the study will benefit all players in the Tanzanian animal feed value chain, firstly feed millers, but also their customers: farmers in the aquaculture, dairy, pig and mainly poultry sectors. Feed producers will be enabled to scale up their businesses through the findings and activities.</narrative>
  </description>
